 It's the first thing on the System Control Board's **Development** page (2026-11-24): everything we've found that needs doing, so nothing is forgotten and no gauge slips. Its gauge reads how many open tasks are on time (a task is **late** when it's a day old and nobody has it); until Jason approves its target (proposed: nothing late, with **Approve the target**) it shows how much of its setup is done, like every gauge. Under it, **Add a task**: choose the gauge or the area it belongs to (or **A new area…**), say what needs doing, why (what it fixes, or who asked) and where it came from (a decision, a hand-off, or whose words and when). The list shows what needs you first, late at the top, the first 10 then **Show all**; **Everything open ▾** filters it (Late, Open, Taken, Being fixed). Each task says where it stands: **Open** (nobody has it yet), **Late**, **Taken** (with whom), **Being fixed** in a named release, and it's **Done** by itself once its stages are through (below), or, for a gauge's **Request a fix**, once its check passes. Its one button is the next step (**Take it**, then **Name its release**); **✎** edits what it says, the old words kept in its **History** (the clock icon); **⋯** has the other moves: **Move to a session…**, **Name its release…** and **Withdraw, with a reason…**. Press a task's name to open its page beside the list (2026-11-24): its **Scope** (goal, in scope, out of scope, done when), its **Priority** (1 first, 2 next, 3 when there's room; open and late tasks are listed by it) and its **Steps**, each saying what to see, what to do and how it's confirmed, with **Confirm it's done**. Every field has **✎** to edit it, the old words kept in the task's **History**; a step confirmed by mistake is corrected with ✎ too. Under **Decisions**, the task names the decision records it rests on, each with its number and title; press one to read it on System Configuration → Decisions beside the board. **Attach a decision** finds one by its number or a word from its title, in the same list that page has; **✎** detaches one, kept in the task's History. The list shows each task's priority, its goal how many of its steps are confirmed and its decisions. Done and withdrawn tasks stay a week under **Done and withdrawn this week**. Since 2026-11-25 each task is one of three types, chosen under **Type** when you add it (Work if you don't choose). A **Work** ticket moves through four stages: **Planning**, **Building**, **Implementation** and **Confirmation**. It moves from Planning to Building with **Move to Building** once it has a goal, a Done when, a decision attached (or **No decision applies**) and someone who has it; until then the button is greyed and the line above it says what's missing. It moves on by itself once its release is named (Implementation), once that release is live (Confirmation), and once every step is confirmed (Done; a task with no steps is Done as soon as its release is live). **✎** beside the stages sends it back one or more stages, with your reason kept in its History. An **Audit** ticket is a finding from the daily check, looked at twice before it reaches anyone. A **Resolution** ticket is a decision only the Founder makes: its **Decision Brief** says the decision, why it needs the Founder, the line it crosses, options A and B with their pros and cons and which is recommended, the impact if it waits, and the cost. Each part has **✎** while it's a draft; **Send to the Founder** once it's whole. It then reads **Waiting for you** at the top of the queue and goes in the daily summary; the Founder answers with **Choose A**, **Choose B** or **Answer in my words**, on the task or in reply to the summary. A new task then records the answer as standing precedent, and the decision closes once that's done. The filter (one button) also shows each type and each stage, with counts, and the round people icon beside it opens the **Team**. Every hour the open tasks reach the session manager with the gauge's requests, and a late one is flagged to it.
 
 ### I'm on the Traversence team. What is the Team page?
-System Configuration → **Team** (2026-11-25) lists the roles that work the Work queue. It has one row for each of the 34 roles in the plan, in four departments: Leadership, Operations, Build and Advocates. **Operators** move the day's work along. **Keepers** guard a rule or a record in the background. **Auditors** confirm work and run the daily check. **Advocates** are each bound to a test account (the Staff Views advocate to the staff test logins): they're a view for checks and never act. The **Founder** is the real login and answers the decisions only the Founder can make; the Founder is never an agent. Each department opens from its own row, and the roles on standby are folded into one group at the end. Press a role's name to open it beside the list. You'll see its mandate, the steps it has, what it acts as, the highest tier it may decide alone (Tier 3 always goes to the Founder), the decisions it works under and its **History**. **✎ Edit its words** changes its name, mandate, what it works on and its rules, and the old words are kept in its History. **Put on standby**, **Retire it** and **Make active** each have their way back on the same card, and a role is never deleted. What a role may do comes only from Permissions, for the level and department it acts as, never from the Team page. A role with no steps yet does no work until one is written for it. The agents work only on the staging copy (see "What does the Team's runner do?"); nothing they do reaches the live site until staging is published in a batch the Founder approved.
+System Configuration → **Team** lists the roles that work the Work queue: the 32 roles of decisions/0180 (2026-11-26) and the 12 test-account advocates. They're grouped by layer: the **Founder** (the real login, who gives the Founder rulings and is never an agent), the **Executive suite** (CEO, COO, CFO, CTO, CIO and CMO), the seven **Middle managers**, each with its own swimlane (Project Manager, Customer Service Manager, Accounting Manager, IT Manager, Editorial Director, Marketing Director and Sales Director), the **Specialists** who do the work in each lane, and the **Advocates**. Each advocate is bound to a test account (the Staff Views advocate to the staff test logins): they're a view for checks and never act. Every role is active. A role put on standby folds into one group at the end, and its work goes to the next active role above it (the Founder, if none is). The roles decisions/0180 replaced aren't listed anywhere; past tickets still show their names. Press a role's name to open it beside the list. You'll see its layer, its mandate, the steps it has, what it acts as, the highest tier it may decide alone (a Founder ruling, Tier 5, always goes to the Founder), the decisions it works under, who it reports to and its **History**. **✎ Edit its words** changes its name, mandate, what it works on and its rules, and the old words are kept in its History. **Put on standby** and **Make active** each have their way back on the same card, and a role is never deleted. What a role may do comes only from Permissions, for the level and department it acts as, never from the Team page; a role with none says **No permissions yet**, and only the Founder gives a role permissions. A role with no steps yet does no work until one is written for it. The agents work only on the staging copy (see "What does the Team's runner do?"); nothing they do reaches the live site until it's checked and published.
 
 ### I'm on the Traversence team. What does the Team's runner do?
-On the staging copy only, and only while its switch is on (System Configuration → Switches there), every ten minutes it takes one ticket from the Work queue and does one step for one working role, then stops. How far it goes is set by **Agents work up to** on the Team page, which stays at **Planning** until the Founder raises it (see "What is the Team's decision tree?"). At Planning, the **Project Manager** proposes moving a Work ticket to Building once its gate passes, and a person's **Accept** makes the move. Above Planning it makes the move itself. The **System Architect** writes a Building ticket's build brief for the session manager, but only once the ceiling is above Planning. The **Executive Translator** proposes the plain words of a Decision Brief from what we hold. It never works a ticket a person has (only unowned ones, or ones a Team role has), never answers a Tier 3 decision (only the Founder does), never writes over words a person wrote (it proposes them instead, and a person's **Accept** fills them in, kept in the History), and never calls an outside service or paid AI. Each step is a line on the ticket's page: who did it, what and when, with **Undo** for a move or a brief (put back as if it never happened; a second Undo is refused) and **Accept** or **Dismiss** for a proposal. What it can do comes only from Permissions for the role's level and department; a step it isn't allowed says so and changes nothing. On the live site it does nothing at all. What it did on staging reaches the live site only when staging is published, in the batch the Founder approved.
